# Throw away the map built so far and start a new one, with nothing restarted.
#
# Served by pgo_node on the relative name `reset_mapping`, so it lands on
# <robot_id>/pgo/reset_mapping. This is the operator-facing entry point (the
# console's "New map" control reaches it through the backend); pgo orchestrates
# the whole sequence so that a caller makes ONE request and gets ONE verdict,
# and cannot get the ordering wrong because no ordering is exposed to it.
#
# What it does, in order: stop accepting incoming cloud/odom pairs, reset the
# LIO front end over ResetLIO, rebuild SimplePGO from scratch, then resume with
# a timestamp gate that discards the old run's tail still sitting in the
# message_filters synchroniser and the cloud buffer. Pausing FIRST is what makes
# it robust -- while the front end's state changes, pgo is not consuming, so the
# odometry discontinuity has nowhere to land.
#
# The only fallible step (the ResetLIO round trip) happens before anything is
# destroyed, so a failure leaves the pose graph exactly as it was and says so.
# There is no half-reset state to recover from.
#
# THE MAP IS NOT SAVED FIRST. pgo holds its keyframes in RAM and save_maps is
# the only thing that serialises them, so whatever this discards is gone for
# good short of replaying a bag. Saving first is the caller's decision, kept
# separate on purpose: the common use is abandoning a run that went wrong in its
# first thirty seconds, where an automatic save would only litter the catalogue.

# Reset the LIO front end too -- which is what "a new map" means, since the pose
# graph's origin follows the odometry origin. False resets the pose graph ALONE,
# leaving pointlio's ikd-tree and odometry running: that is a bag-replay and
# debugging affordance (a fresh graph over an unchanged odometry stream), and it
# is NOT what the operator console sends.
bool reset_lio
---
bool success
string message

# What pointlio reported as its last published odometry stamp, echoed so a
# caller can correlate logs across the two nodes. 0.0 when reset_lio was false,
# or when the front end had not reached MAPPING.
float64 lio_last_odom_time

# How many keyframes were discarded. The receipt that something was actually
# thrown away: zero means the run had banked nothing yet, which is a materially
# different outcome from resetting a twenty-minute drive and worth logging.
uint32 dropped_key_poses
